Sure, I'd be happy to help you create a function that supports accessibility features such as screen reader support. Here's an example using JavaScript and the `window.navigator` object to check for screen reader support. This function will add ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes to various elements to make them more accessible to screen reader users.

```javascript
function makeAccessible(element) {
  // Check for screen reader support
  if ('screenReaderType' in window.navigator) {
    // Add ARIA attributes based on screen reader type
    const screenReaderType = window.navigator.screenReaderType;

    if (screenReaderType === 'default' || screenReaderType === 'system') {
      // For default or system screen readers, add ARIA roles and properties
      element.setAttribute('aria-label', element.getAttribute('title') || '');
      element.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', 'accessible-label');

      // For example, if the element is a button, add a 'role' attribute
      if (element.tagName === 'BUTTON') {
        element.setAttribute('role', 'button');
      }
    } else if (screenReaderType === 'user') {
      // For user-defined screen readers, add ARIA properties
      element.setAttribute('aria-describedby', 'accessible-description');
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const button = document.getElementById('my-button');
makeAccessible(button);
```

This function checks for screen reader support using the `screenReaderType` property of the `window.navigator` object. Based on the screen reader type, it adds ARIA attributes such as `aria-label`, `aria-labelledby`, and `aria-describedby` to elements. For example, if the element is a button, it adds a `role` attribute to make it more accessible to screen reader users.

You can customize this function to add additional ARIA attributes based on your specific needs. Make sure to test your application with various screen readers to ensure that it is accessible to all users.
